1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
A declaration tells the compiler the name, the parameter types and the return type. A definition also supplies the body.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:16,000
With a prototype in view, the compiler converts each argument to the declared parameter type, as if by assignment.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Without a prototype the compiler cannot convert. Only the default argument promotions apply: char and short become int, float becomes double.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
So a char parameter that receives a promoted int is undefined behaviour. The code still compiles, and the answer is not yours to predict.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Every argument is copied into a new parameter object. The parameter is not the caller's variable.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:46,000
So a swap that swaps its own parameters changes nothing in main. The two copies die when the call returns.

7
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:53,000
An array parameter is the same rule in disguise: a parameter declared int a[] is adjusted to int *a. The pointer is copied, not the array.

8
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:00,000
That is why sizeof inside the function gives the size of a pointer, not the size of the caller's array.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Scope is where a name is visible. C has block scope, function scope, file scope and function-prototype scope.

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Name lookup starts in the innermost block and moves outward. The first declaration found wins.

11
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:24,000
So a local hides a global of the same name. That is shadowing, and it is what makes a two-variable trace confusing.

12
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
In the 2023 question, main's x and the static x inside funcp are different objects that happen to share a spelling.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Scope and lifetime are different questions. Scope asks where the name is visible; lifetime asks when the object exists.

14
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:46,000
An automatic object is created when its block is entered and destroyed when it is left. Each call gets a fresh one.

15
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:54,000
A static object exists for the whole run and is initialised once, before main. A call does not recreate it.

16
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
So a static local can count its own calls. Its name is still local; only its lifetime is global.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:08,000
Here is the counter: static int n = 0; then n++; then return n. Three calls return 1, 2, 3.

18
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:16,000
The 2019 paper hides the same idea in a loop header: for (r(); r(); r()), with static int num = 7; return num--;

19
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:24,000
The initialisation runs once, then condition, body, update, condition. Each r() returns the value and then decrements.

20
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
The body prints 5, then 2. The answer is 52, and the page computes it rather than trusting the trace.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Linkage is about the linker, not the block. External linkage means the name can be used from another translation unit.

22
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:46,000
static at file scope gives internal linkage, so the name stays in this file. extern says the object exists, here or elsewhere.

23
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:54,000
The four storage classes fix three things at once: storage duration, linkage, and the default initial value.

24
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Automatic objects start as garbage. Objects with static duration start as zero. That one difference answers many questions.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:08,000
#define is a text substitution done before the compiler sees the code. SQR(x) (x*x) with b+2 becomes b+2*b+2.

26
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:16,000
With b = 4 that is 4 + 2*4 + 2 = 14, not 36. Multiplication binds tighter than addition.

27
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:23,000
The fix is two pairs of parentheses: around each parameter, and around the whole body. ((x)*(x)) gives 36.

28
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:30,000
The same trap turns 64 / SQR(4) into 64/4*4 = 64 instead of 4. Division and multiplication share a level and run left to right.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:38,000
A macro argument is substituted once for every use in the body. Two uses mean two evaluations.

30
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:46,000
With i = 5, SQR(i++) expands to ((i++)*(i++)). The first gives 5, the second gives 6, so the product is 30.

31
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:53,000
A function evaluates each argument once, because the argument is evaluated to produce the copy. That is the difference.

32
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:00,000
So use a macro for a constant or a type-generic wrapper, and a function when an argument can have a side effect.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:08,000
#include is textual: the whole header is pasted in at that line. Nothing checks whether it was already included.

34
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:16,000
Include the same header twice and its struct definition appears twice, which is an error. An include guard prevents it.

35
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:23,000
#ifndef GUARD, #define GUARD, then the body, then #endif. On the second inclusion the body expands to nothing.

36
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Conditional compilation chooses code before compiling. NDEBUG turns assert into nothing, so a debug check can vanish in a release build.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:38,000
The build is four steps: preprocess, compile, assemble, link. Each one has its own kind of error.

38
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:46,000
The preprocessor expands macros and pastes includes. The compiler turns one translation unit into assembly. The assembler makes an object file.

39
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:53,000
The linker joins the object files and resolves names. One external definition per name is the rule.

40
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Two non-static definitions of one global collide at link time. Two static ones do not, because each is private to its file.

